<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Helicon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Helicon is a mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, rising to about 1,750 metres between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-copais">Lake Copais</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. In Greek myth it was sacred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>, whose sanctuary in the Valley of the Muses held festival contests, and the spring Hippocrene was said to have been struck from it by the hoof of Pegasus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> tells that the Muses gave him his poetic gift there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-helicon.jpg" alt="Mount Helicon" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Skene of Rubislaw · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Helicon,_Parnassus_and_Plain_of_Thebes_-_Skene_James_-_1838-1845.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mount Helicon lies in the region of Thespiai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> and stands 1,749 metres high. It is located approximately 10 kilometres from the north coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. Some researchers maintain that Helicon was also the Greek name of a mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, Rocca Salvatesta, since a river that started from it was also called Helikon. The monastery of Hosios Loukas, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, stands on the mountain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Greek myth placed two springs sacred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a> on Helicon, the Aganippe and the Hippocrene, both of which carry the Greek word for horse, hippos, in their names. In a related myth the Hippocrene burst from the rock when the winged horse Pegasus struck it with his hoof.</p><p>Poets returned to the mountain again and again. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>, in the late eighth or early seventh century BCE, placed a reference to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a> on Helicon at the start of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a>. The Homeric Hymn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, generally dated to the seventh century, hails the god as Lord of Helicon. In his Aitia the third-century BCE poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a> tells of a dream in which he was young again and talked with the Muses there, explicitly following Hesiod, and he set on Helicon the episode in which Tiresias came upon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> bathing and was blinded but given the art of prophecy. The Roman poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, in his Metamorphoses, writes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a> visiting the Muses on the mountain.</p><p>Cult centres were established in the Valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>, a fertile valley near Thespiai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascra">Ascra</a>, under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s texts, in Hellenistic times if not before. Pausanias visited them in the second century CE and described the sacred grove by the spring Aganippe, with its statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and famed poets and an image of Linus in a small rock worked into the manner of a cave. The tripod Hesiod won at a contest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> was still on view there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The image of Helicon fixed by the Roman poets became an emblem of cultural inspiration again with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and is often referred to in poetry. John Milton calls it the Aonian mount at the opening of Paradise Lost, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torquato-tasso">Torquato Tasso</a> names it Elicona in the second verse of Gerusalemme Liberata. The Hungarian composer Leó Festetics held Helicon balls at his Festetics Palace near Keszthely and named the library he founded the Helikon Library.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Helicon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pindar of Thebes was the greatest choral lyric poet of the fifth century, Corinna of Tanagra was remembered as his rival, and the much earlier poet Hesiod had lived at Ascra below <b>Mount Helicon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Both hymns call the Muses down from <b>Mount Helicon</b> to Delphi to sing of Apollo, and tell the god's story: his birth on Delos, his killing of the serpent that guarded the oracle's tripod, and his defence of Delphi against an invading army of Gauls.</blockquote><a href="/songs/delphic-hymns">Delphic Hymns</a></li><li><blockquote>Thespiae lay in the plain of southern Boeotia below <b>Mount Helicon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/thespiae">Thespiae</a></li></ul></section>
